    "stem": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Scientists have invented a way of producing food by artificial photosynthesis.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 9
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "Solar panels convert sunlight energy into electricity.",
          "The electricity powers an electrolysis reaction between carbon dioxide gas and water to form"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "the organic product acetate.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 9
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "The single-celled alga <i>Chlamydomonas</i>, a protoctist, can use acetate to grow and reproduce"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "in the dark, instead of photosynthesising in the light.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 9
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "The algae can be processed to make a food product."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Fig. 4.1 shows an outline of the artificial photosynthesis process.",
        "source": {
          "qp": 9
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "figure",
        "id": "fig4.1",
        "caption": "Fig. 4.1",
        "src": "figures/q4_fig4_1.png",
        "alt": "Artificial photosynthesis",
        "source": {
          "qp": 9
        }
      }
    ],
    "parts": [
      {
        "key": "a",
        "label": "(a)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 9,
          "ms": 11
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Identify <b>three</b> similarities between the artificial photosynthesis process shown in Fig. 4.1 and the normal process of photosynthesis.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 9
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 3,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "13.1(2)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 3,
          "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>three</i></b><i> from: </i> <i>(both processes) </i>",
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "<b>1 </b> use / require, carbon dioxide / CO<sub>2</sub> <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "<b>2 </b> use / require, water / H<sub>2</sub>O <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "text": "<b>3 </b> use / require, (sun) light energy / source of energy is light <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 4,
              "text": "<b>4 </b> make an organic, molecule / product <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 5,
              "text": "<b>5 </b> produce / release, oxygen / O<sub>2</sub> <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 6,
              "text": "<b>6 </b> involve <u>transduc</u>tion of energy (from one form to another)<b> ; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 7,
              "text": "<b>7 </b> involve electron flow <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "ATP supplies energy and reduced NADP supplies reducing power to the light-independent stage. Compare processes by their inputs, energy transfers and organic products rather than by their names alone.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:13.1(2)",
                "ms:a#1"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Identify the source of energy and carbon.",
            "Trace their conversion into organic products."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Confusing reduced NADP with the carbon source for photosynthesis",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:13.1(2)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
      {
        "key": "b",
        "label": "(b)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 10,
          "ms": 11
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fig. 4.2 shows the single-celled alga <i>Chlamydomonas</i>. It contains a large, cup-shaped chloroplast for photosynthesis.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "figure",
            "id": "fig4.2",
            "caption": "Fig. 4.2",
            "src": "figures/q4_fig4_2.png",
            "alt": "Chlamydomonas cell",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Structures <b>A</b> and <b>B</b> in Fig. 4.2 are found within the chloroplast.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Name structures <b>A</b> and <b>B</b>.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "answerLines",
            "labels": [
              "A",
              "B"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "marks": 2,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "13.1(1)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 2,
          "instruction": null,
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "<b>A</b>  starch, grain / granule <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "<b>B </b> thylakoid (membrane) / lamella / granum <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Chloroplast compartments support different stages of photosynthesis. Membranes provide surfaces for light-dependent reactions, while the stroma contains enzymes of carbon fixation.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:13.1(1)",
                "ms:b#1"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Identify the labelled structure by its position and appearance.",
            "Link the structure to its particular function."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Locating Calvin-cycle enzymes in the thylakoid space",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:13.1(1)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
      {
        "key": "c",
        "label": "(c)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 10
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Thylakoid membranes are the site of the light-dependent stage of photosynthesis.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10
            }
          }
        ],
        "parts": [
          {
            "key": "c.i",
            "label": "(i)",
            "source": {
              "qp": 10,
              "ms": 11
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Name the products of the light-dependent stage of photosynthesis.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 10
                }
              }
            ],
            "marks": 1,
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "13.1(9)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 1,
              "instruction": null,
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "ATP <u>and</u><b> </b>reduced NADP (and oxygen) <b>; </b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                }
              ]
            },
            "er": [],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "Water supplies replacement electrons, while photoactivation raises electron energy. ATP and reduced NADP transfer energy and reducing power to the Calvin cycle.",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(9)",
                    "ms:c.i#1"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Follow water-derived electrons through the two photosystems.",
                "Identify the products and their roles in the next stage."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": [
                {
                  "text": "Describing light as the source of the electrons",
                  "basis": "inferred",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(9)"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "type": "short"
          },
          {
            "key": "c.ii",
            "label": "(ii)",
            "source": {
              "qp": 11,
              "ms": 11
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Some of the protein components of the thylakoid membrane have a role in the light-dependent stage of photosynthesis.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 11
                }
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Explain the roles of the different proteins that function in the light-dependent stage of photosynthesis.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 11
                }
              }
            ],
            "marks": 4,
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "13.1(10)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 4,
              "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>four</i></b><i> from: </i>",
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "<b>1 </b> electron carriers / electron transport chain / to carry electrons <b>; </b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 2,
                  "text": "<b>2 </b> to release energy to, move / pump, protons (into, thylakoid space / lumen) <b>or </b> to release energy to generate proton gradient <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 3,
                  "text": "<b>3 </b> oxygen-evolving, complex / enzyme <b>; </b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 4,
                  "text": "<b>4</b>  (to catalyse) splitting / photolysis, of water <b>; </b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 5,
                  "text": "<b>5 </b> LHC / antenna complex / photosystem, <u>proteins</u> hold pigments <b>;</b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                },
                {
                  "n": 6,
                  "text": "<b>6 </b> ATP synth(et)ase to make ATP <b>; </b>",
                  "numberedInMs": false
                }
              ]
            },
            "er": [],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "The proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane couples electron transport to ATP synthesis. Proton return is towards the stroma, where ATP is released.",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(10)",
                    "ms:c.ii#1"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Identify how protons accumulate in the thylakoid space.",
                "Trace their return through ATP synthase."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": [
                {
                  "text": "Reversing the direction of proton return through ATP synthase",
                  "basis": "inferred",
                  "refs": [
                    "syllabus:13.1(10)"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "type": "short"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "key": "d",
        "label": "(d)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 11,
          "ms": 11
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The products of the light-dependent stage of photosynthesis are used in the Calvin cycle. Calvin cycle intermediates are used to produce amino acids, carbohydrates and lipids.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 11
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Name the Calvin cycle intermediate that can be used to produce starch.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 11
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 1,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "13.1(12)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 1,
          "instruction": null,
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "triose phosphate <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Calvin-cycle intermediates can be diverted into biosynthesis. Some TP must also remain available to regenerate RuBP so that carbon fixation continues.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:13.1(12)",
                "ms:d#1"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Identify the intermediate required by the question.",
            "Distinguish biosynthetic use from regeneration of the acceptor."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Assuming that all TP leaves the Calvin cycle",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:13.1(12)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
